WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.772
Performance issues in local inference often fall into two categories:

00:00:03.772 --> 00:00:05.976
slow prompt processing and slow generation.

00:00:05.976 --> 00:00:10.382
Prompt processing is the work of reading and embedding the input context.

00:00:10.382 --> 00:00:12.586
Generation is the token-by-token output step.

00:00:12.586 --> 00:00:15.524
A setup may load quickly but generate slowly,

00:00:15.524 --> 00:00:19.196
or handle short prompts well but struggle with long context.

00:00:19.196 --> 00:00:22.501
Knowing which phase is slow helps narrow the cause.

00:00:22.501 --> 00:00:25.806
Memory pressure is one of the most common problems.

00:00:25.806 --> 00:00:30.580
A model, quantization, or context length may exceed available VRAM or system RAM.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:34.620
GPU offload settings may be too aggressive, causing failures or instability.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.190
CPU-only execution may work but feel slow.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:41.230
Disk speed can affect model loading time, especially with large files.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.800
Batch size and thread settings can help,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.207
but they should be adjusted carefully and tested with the actual workload.

00:00:48.207 --> 00:00:53.715
Sampling settings affect output behavior and can be mistaken for performance or model quality issues.

00:00:53.715 --> 00:00:55.184
Temperature, top-p, repetition controls,

00:00:55.184 --> 00:00:59.224
and related options influence how deterministic or varied the output feels.

00:00:59.224 --> 00:01:03.998
If results seem rambling, repetitive, or inconsistent, the issue may be sampling configuration,

00:01:03.998 --> 00:01:06.568
prompt formatting, model fit, or quantization quality.

00:01:06.568 --> 00:01:09.506
Troubleshooting should separate runtime settings from model capability.

00:01:09.506 --> 00:01:14.647
A practical troubleshooting loop starts with a known compatible runtime, a trusted model file,

00:01:14.647 --> 00:01:17.218
conservative context length, and a documented baseline.

00:01:17.218 --> 00:01:22.359
Then change one variable at a time: quantization, GPU offload, thread count, context length,

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:23.461
or sampling settings.

00:01:23.461 --> 00:01:26.032
Compare memory use, speed, and output quality.

00:01:26.032 --> 00:01:28.969
This disciplined approach is slower than random tweaking,

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:31.540
but it produces knowledge you can reuse.
